the ice shelves around Antarctica are  one of the largest uncertainties in  future climate predictions we don't  really know how stable or unstable they  are and if they're unstable they can  contribute incredible amounts of water  to global sea level rise one of the  reasons that we don't understand them  quite as well as we'd like to is they're  really hard to measure  boaty mcboatface is an autonomous  underwater vehicle it's about 800  kilograms and 3 or 4 metres across looks  like a little yellow submarine  it makes measurements of ocean currents  the temperature and salinity in the  water and then the small scale  turbulence that's active in the water as  well it's an autonomous vehicle which  means that it's capable of making  measurements along a predetermined path  that somebody has programmed into it and  then finally when it's completed its  mission come back to the surface to be  recovered and half the data downloaded  when we went out a couple years ago this  was the first time it was deployed for a  science mission and so everyone was  really excited to see what would happen  and then even more exciting I would say  was when it came back with a whole suite  of data that we could then use to look  at what was going on near the seabed  so one of the potential use cases for  auto sub or for the bode mcboatface  autonomous vehicles would be to actually  transit all the way under Arctic sea ice  and come out the other side with a whole  wealth of data that we haven't  previously observed measuring under the  sea ice in any region and in the Arctic  which is so broadly covered is is a real  challenge for traditional measurements  so what we're trying to do is increase  the endurance of the vehicle as well as  enable it to navigate for long periods  of time without having reference points  being able to go under sea ice gives us  a really unique data set to try and nail  down how much these things may be  contributing to future sea level rise  you
 

